 for about half of the long tail of badly managed firms and up to two thirds of the American advantage over Europe in management practices.\nL122: * * *\nL123: \nL124: March 2006     Paper Number CEPDP0716\nL125: \nL126: 【69†Download PDF - Measuring and Explaining Management Practices Across Firms and Countries】\nL127: \nL128: 【70†Download Data】\nL129: \nL130: This CEP discussion paper is published under the centre's 【5†Growth programme】.\nL131: ###### Related\nL132: \nL133: 【71†'Measuring and Explaining Management Practices Across Firms and Nations', Nick Bloom and John Van Reenen, Quarterly Journal of Economics (2007) 122 (4), 1351-1408. Read more...†watermark.silverchair.com】\nL134: ######\nL135: \nL136: Other links\nL137: \nL138: 【72†management practices】; 【73†productivity】; 【74†competition】; 【75†family firms】\nL139: \nL140: 【76†Search All Discussion papers】\nL141: \nL142: 【21†Search All Publications】\nL143: \nL144: CEP is co-funded by the LSE and ESRC, part of UKRI:\nL145: \nL146: * * *\nL147: \nL148: Centre for Economic Performance\nL149: London School of Economics and Political Science\nL150: Houghton Street\nL151: London\nL152: WC2A 2AE\nL153: UK\nL154: \nL155: Follow us:\nL156: \nL157: * * *\nL158: \nL159: * * *\nL160: \nL161: Copyright © CEP & LSE 2005 - 2026\nL162: \nL163: LSE is a private company limited by guarantee, registration number 70527\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn2414view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.cepal.org/es/notas/impactos-economicos-envejecimiento\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.cepal.org/es/notas/impactos-economicos-envejecimiento: (403) Forbidden--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPrint (https://remittanceprices.worldbank.org/sites/default/files/2026-04/RPW_main_report_and_annex_Q325.pdf)\nciteturn2414view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 32; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2411view5\",\"lineno\":130}); Total lines: 940\nL65@P2: 367 country corridors worldwide. RPW tracks the cost of sending remittances for four main RSP types:\nL66@P2: Banks, MTOs, Mobile Operators, and Post Offices. MTOs include both traditional providers and\nL67@P2: innovative/fintech players. On average, 21.7 services per corridor are tracked.\nL71@P3: APIs were not employed. Due to this deviation in data collection methodology, these services were\nL72@P3: not included in the main analysis in Q3 2025; instead, the data are presented in Annex V.\nL73@P3: • In Q3 2025, five hundred and nineteen services were moved from the prospects index to the main\nL74@P3: analysis, after demonstrating consistency, materiality and diversity. In the same quarter, 138 new\nL75@P3: services were added to the prospects index. RPW will continue to monitor and include new services\nL76@P3-4: when and where relevant using the prospects index.Remittance Prices Worldwide - Issue 54, September 2025\nL77@P4: 5\nL78@P4: Official Use Only\nL79@P4: Table of Contents\nL80@P4: Overview...........................................................................................................................3\nL81@P4: Key findings ......................................................................................................................3\nL82@P4: Progress tracker ...............................................................................................................6\nL83@P4: Prospects index ................................................................................................................6\nL84@P4: Global trends ....................................................................................................................6\nL85@P4: Global average experiences a decrease ...............................................................................................................6\nL86@P4: International MTO index.....................................................................................................................................8\nL87@P4: Global weighted average.....................................................................................................................................8\nL88@P4: Smart Remitter Target (SmaRT).........................................................................................................................9\nL89@P4: Trends in corridor average total costs ...............................................................................................................10\nL90@P4: G8 and G20 countries.....................................................................................................11\nL91@P4: Cost of sending remittances from G8 countries................................................................................................11\nL92@P4: Cost of sending remittances from and to G20 countries...................................................................................12\nL93@P4: Regional trends ..............................................................................................................14\nL94@P4: Cost of sending remittances by region of the world..........................................................................................14\nL95@P4: Regional differences in speed of sending remittances ......................................................................................15\nL96@P4: Cost structure .................................................................................................................16\nL97@P4: Cash vs. digital services ....................................................................................................................................16\nL98@P4: Costs by RSP type .............................................................................................................................................17\nL99@P4: Figure B2.1 Average cost of sending $200 via mobile operators.....................................................................19\nL100@P4: Costs by sending and receiving method............................................................................................................19\nL101@P4: Annex I – Tables ($200) .................................................................................................21\nL102@P4: Annex II - Cost Trends for Sending $500........................................................................23\nL103@P4: Global trends for sending $500 .........................................................................................................................23\nL104@P4: Global average total cost....................................................................................................................................................23\nL105@P4: International MTO Index ....................................................................................................................................................24\nL106@P4: SmaRT Remitter Indicator ..................................................................................................................................................24\nL107@P4: G20 Countries ...................................................................................................................................................24\nL108@P4: Cost of sending remittances from and to G20 countries ...................................................................................................24\nL109@P4: Annex III – Tables ($500)................................................................................................27\nL110@P4: Annex IV – Prospects Index............................................................................................28\nL111@P4-5: Annex V – Corridors from Russia....................................................................................31Remittance Prices Worldwide - Issue 54, September 2025\nL112@P5: 6\nL113@P5: Official Use Only\nL114@P5: Progress tracker\nL115@P5: RPW indicators are used to measure the progress towards targets of global efforts for the reduction of\nL116@P5: remittance costs. The UN SDGs and the G20 have indicated a target of 3 percent for the Global Average\nL117@P5: to be reached by 2030.1 At the same time, the UN SDGs and the G20 have also committed to ensuring\nL118@P5: that in all corridors, remittances can be transferred for 5 percent or less.\nL119@P5: The figure below summarizes the progress towards these three targets.\nL120@P5: Prospects index\nL121@P5: Since Q1 2022, RPW data collection has been used to also monitor new services via a prospects\nL122@P5: index. These new services are candidates for inclusion in the main index in the future subject to them\nL123@P5: meeting certain requirements. In Q3 2025, 519 services were moved from the prospects index to the\nL124@P5: main analysis, after demonstrating consistency, materiality and diversity. In the same quarter, 138 new\nL125@P5: services were added to the prospects index. Please see Annex IV for details.\nL126@P5: Global trends\nL127@P5: Global average experiences a decrease\nL128@P5: In Q3 2025, the Global Average cost for sending remittances was 6.36 percent, a decrease from 6.49 in\nL129@P5: 1 The G8 (L’Aquila, 2010) and the G20 (Cannes, 2011 and Brisbane, 2014) committed to reduce the Global Average Total\nL130@P5: Cost to 5 percent. The G20 aligned with the UN’s 2030 Agenda to include cost reduction targets set under SDG 10.c. In\nL131@P5: addition, through the G20 Roadmap for Enhancing Cross-Border Payments, the G20 reaffirmed SDG 10.c for remittance\nL132@P5: costs. World Bank’s Remittance Prices Worldwide database will be used to monitor several targets on remittances in the\nL133@P5: Roadmap, including those on cost, speed and transparency. See FSB (2021), Targets for addressing the four challenges of\nL134@P5: cross-border payments (https://www.fsb.org/wp-content/uploads/P131021-2.pdf) and FSB (2022), Developing the\nL135@P5: Implementation Approach for the Cross-Border Payments Targets (https://www.fsb.org/wp-content/uploads/P171122.pdf).\nL136@P5: Until now, RPW has been reporting on the G8/G20 remittance target of 5%. In line with the G20 affirmation of the SDG 10.c\nL137@P5: remittance price targets, starting with Q1 2023 edition of the RPW Quarterly Reports, the reporting is being adjusted to keep\nL138@P5-6: only SDG 10.c. There has been no change in the methodology.Remittance Prices Worldwide - Issue 54, September 2025\nL139@P6: 7\nL140@P6: Official Use Only\nL141@P6: Q1 2025. The Global Average has remained below 7.00 percent since Q1 2019 (see Figure 1 below and\nL142@P6: Table 1 in the Annex). Overall, this represents a decline of 3.31 percentage points since Q1 2009, when\nL143@P6: the figure was recorded at 9.67 percent. In Q3 2025, the global average for digital remittances was\nL144@P6: recorded at 4.59 percent, while the global average for non-digital remittances was 7.30 percent. Digital\nL145@P6: services account for 35% of all services RPW collected in Q3 2025.\nL146@P6: 2\nL147@P6: Figure 1 Trends in the global cost of sending $200 in remittances3\nL148@P6: 2 A digital remittance must be sent via a payment instrument in an online or self-assisted manner, and received into a transaction account,\nL149@P6: i.e., bank account, transaction account maintained at a non-bank deposit taking institution (say a post office), mobile money or e-money\nL150@P6: account.\nL151@P6: 3 Figures for the global average were adjusted in Q1 2014 following a clean-up of the entire database. Some values slightly vary from figures\nL152@P6-7: published prior to Q1 2014.Remittance Prices Worldwide - Issue 54, September 2025\nL153@P7: 8\nL154@P7: Official Use Only\nL155@P7: International MTO index\nL156@P7: The International MTO Index tracks the prices of MTOs that are present in at least 85 percent of corridors\nL157@P7: covered in the RPW database.4In Q3 2025, the International MTO Index recorded a decrease to 5.52\nL158@P7: percent from the previous value of 5.91 percent in Q1 2025. Over the year, this figure was down by 1.13\nL159@P7: of a percentage point, recorded at 6.65 percent in Q3 2024. This figure has come down by 4.84 percentage\nL160@P7: points from its first recorded value of 10.36 percent in Q1 2009. In Q3 2025, the digital-only MTO Index\nL161@P7: recorded at 3.54 percent, a minute decrease from 3.55 percent in Q1 2025.\nL162@P7: 5 The digital-only MTO Index\nL163@P7: has consistently remained below both the International MTO Index and the global average.\nL164@P7: Figure 2 Trends in International MTO Index & Digital-only MTO Index\nL165@P7: Global weighted average\nL166@P7: The global weighted average total cost accounts for the relative size of the flows in each remittance\nL167@P7: 4 The International MTOs Index includes all MTOs that are present in over 85 percent of RPW corridors. Thus far, it has included Western\nL168@P7: Union and MoneyGram, which operate in 95 percent and 90 percent of the country corridors covered in the database, respectively.\nL169@P7: 5 A digital-only MTO refers to money transfer operators that send remittances predominantly through digital channels. The digital-only MTO\nL170@P7-8: index includes five digital-only MTOs, Wise, Remitly, WorldRemit, InstaReM and Xoom. Some of these providers also have physical channels.Remittance Prices Worldwide - Issue 54, September 2025\nL171@P8: 9\nL172@P8: Official Use Only\nL173@P8: corridor.6 This, as illustrated in Figure 3 (see also Table 1 in the Annex), has at times shown a different\nL174@P8: pattern from the simple average. The Global Weighted Average was recorded at 5.04 percent in Q3 2025.\nL175@P8: Figure 3 Trends in Global weighted average & SmaRT average\nL176@P8: Smart Remitter Target (SmaRT)\nL177@P8: To complement the Global Average and Global Weighted Averages described above, the World Bank\nL178@P8: introduced the SmaRT indicator in Q2 2016, which aims to reflect the cost that a savvy consumer with\nL179@P8: access to sufficiently complete information could pay to transfer remittances in each corridor. SmaRT is\nL180@P8: calculated as the simple average of the three cheapest qualifying services for sending the equivalent of\nL181@P8: $200 in each corridor and is expressed as a percentage of the total amount sent. In addition to\nL182@P8: transparency, services must meet additional criteria to qualify for being included in the SmaRT calculation,\nL183@P8: including transaction speed (five days or less), and accessibility, determined by geographic proximity of\nL184@P8: 6\nL185@P8: It is important to note that, while official data on remittance flows by bilateral corridors are currently not available, estimates (Ratha and\nL186@P8: Shaw 2007, the 2018 version, available at\nL187@P8: http://www.worldbank.org/en/topic/migrationremittancesdiasporaissues/brief/migration-remittances-data) have been used in this calculation.\nL188@P8: These estimates are based on the Balance of Payments (BOP) and factor in migrant stocks, destination country incomes, and source country\nL189@P8: incomes. The methodology for these estimates has been questioned, as well as the accuracy of official data on remittance flows and migrant\nL190@P8: stocks. However, this still represents the only available comprehensive dataset on bilateral remittance flows. It also seems likely that overall,\nL191@P8: the dataset is sufficiently accurate to reflect at least the proportion between the different corridors, hence offering a good approximation to\nL192@P8-9: weight the relevance of each corridor in terms of flow size.Remittance Prices Worldwide - Issue 54, September 2025\nL193@P9: 10\nL194@P9: Official Use Only\nL195@P9: branches for services that require physical presence, or access to any technology or device necessary to\nL196@P9: use the service, such as a bank account, mobile phone, or the Internet.7In Q3 2025, the Global SmaRT\nL197@P9: Average remained the same at 3.29 percent.\nL198@P9: The potential of SmaRT can be appreciated even more at the corridor level, where the indicators can\nL199@P9: inform policy actions by identifying limitations at a more granular level. In Q3 2025, 20 of the 3498 corridors\nL200@P9: did not have any SmaRT qualifying services, indicating that in these corridors there is an issue with either\nL201@P9: access or reach of services, or a lower level of competition. Of these 20 corridors, nine are destined for\nL202@P9: Sub-Saharan Africa, eight for the Middle East, North Africa, Afghanistan & Pakistan, one for South Asia,\nL203@P9: one for Europe & Central Asia, and one for Latin America and the Caribbean. An overwhelming majority\nL204@P9: of these corridors have low Internet penetration and/or have low transaction account penetration, which\nL205@P9: indicate that access to the Internet and accounts should be proactively targeted in these regions as a\nL206@P9: means of encouraging more diverse payment and remittance services offering. Majority of the receiving\nL207@P9: countries in this group have a “Green Light” SmaRT rating for accessibility to Cash (measured by\nL208@P9: availability of cash services), and majority (14 out of 20 – see Box 1) have a “Green Light” rating for Mobile\nL209@P9: services (measured by number of cellular subscriptions per 100 persons).\nL210@P9: Box 1 - Corridors without SmaRT qualifying services with “Green Light” rating for mobile\nL211@P9: services\nL212@P9: Angola to Namibia\nL213@P9: Germany to Tajikistan\nL214@P9: Jordan to Syrian Arab Rep.\nL215@P9: Qatar to Egypt, Arab Rep\nL216@P9: Saudi Arabia to Sudan\nL217@P9: Belgium to Algeria\nL218@P9: Ghana to Nigeria\nL219@P9: Netherlands to Suriname\nL220@P9: Qatar to Sudan\nL221@P9: United Arab Emirates to Sudan\nL222@P9: France to Algeria\nL223@P9: India to Sri Lanka\nL224@P9: Pakistan to Afghanistan\nL225@P9: Saudi Arabia to Syrian Arab Rep.\nL226@P9: Note: In Q3 2025, RPW continued to experience difficulties in collecting data on all outbound services from Pakistan. In\nL227@P9: addition, data on services originating in the Russian Federation (9 corridors) were not included. Therefore, for Q3 2025,\nL228@P9: RPW reports no qualifying services in these 10 corridors, one of which is listed in this Box in italics.\nL229@P9: The UN SDGs committed to ensure that, by 2030, it should be possible to send remittances for 5 percent\nL230@P9: or less in every corridor. The SmaRT averages are used as a reference for this indicator, reflecting the\nL231@P9: fact that in any given corridor there are services available to customers that meet the requirements\nL232@P9: described above, while also on average offering a cost that is in line with the UN SDG. As of Q3 2025, 78\nL233@P9: percent of all corridors covered in the RPW database had SmaRT corridor averages below 5 percent.9\nL234@P9: Trends in corridor average total costs\nL235@P9: 7 For additional information on the methodology used to calculate SmaRT see\nL236@P9: https://remittanceprices.worldbank.org/sites/default/files/smart_methodology.pdf\nL237@P9: 8 The number of corridors excludes the 9 corridors originating in Russia.\nL238@P9-10: 9 Please note that this figure excludes the 9 corridors which originate in Russia. Remittance Prices Worldwide - Issue 54, September 2025\nL239@P10: 11\nL240@P10: Official Use Only\nL241@P10: Figure 4 shows that compared to Q1 2009, the proportion of corridors with average costs of less than 10\nL242@P10: percent has increased considerably, showing an overall increase of share by 31 percentage points (53\nL243@P10: percent of corridors in Q1 2009, compared to 83 percent of corridors in Q3 2025). This shift is naturally\nL244@P10: accompanied by a decrease of share of corridors exhibiting total costs over 15 percent (18 percent of\nL245@P10: corridors compared to 5 percent of corridors in Q3 2025). In the 10-15 percent total cost category there\nL246@P10: are 9 percent of corridors in Q3 2025, compared to 29 percent of corridors in Q1 2009. Year over year\nL247@P10: since Q1 2019, this gradual shift is visible. Of the 13 corridors with costs above 20 percent in Q3 2025,\nL248@P10: nine of them originate from Sub-Saharan Africa. In the figure below, corridor average total costs for all\nL249@P10: outbound services from Pakistan remain unavailable. Corridor average total costs for services originating\nL250@P10: from Russia are excluded. These 10 corridors are shown as “NA” in Figure 4.\nL251@P10: Figure 4 Distribution of Average Total Costs\nL252@P10: G8 and G20 countries\nL253@P10: Cost of sending remittances from G8 countries\nL254@P10: The G8 countries include several of the major remittance sending countries in the world.10 The average\nL255@P10: cost for sending remittances from the G8 countries decreased from 5.99 percent in Q1 2025 to 5.40\nL256@P10: percent in Q3 2025. Over the year, this figure decreased by 0.74 of a percentage point (from 6.14 percent\nL257@P10: in Q3 2024). The largest increase in total average cost to send remittances is seen in Japan (from 7.11\nL258@P10: percent to 9.98 percent), followed by France (from 5.14 percent to 5.23 percent). The largest decrease in\nL259@P10: 10 As the dataset did not include data on corridors originating from Russia collected in the same way as from other sending countries, the\nL260@P10-11: G8 and G20 figures reported do not include data on these corridors from Russia. Remittance Prices Worldwide - Issue 54, September 2025\nL261@P11: 12\nL262@P11: Official Use Only\nL263@P11: total average cost to send remittances is seen in Germany (from 6.02 percent to 4.49 percent), followed\nL264@P11: by Italy (from 8.45 percent to 7.44 percent), Canada (from 5.98 percent to 5.27 percent), United Kingdom\nL265@P11: (from 5.20 percent to 4.61 percent), and the United States (from 5.56 percent to 5.04 percent).\nL266@P11: Figure 5 Total average over time in G8 countries\nL267@P11: Cost of sending remittances from and to G20 countries\nL268@P11: The cost of remitting from G20 countries experienced a decrease to 6.39 percent in Q3 2025, as shown\nL269@P11: in Figure 6 (also see Table 3 in the Annex).\nL270@P11-12: Figure 6 Average cost of sending $200 from G8 and G20 countriesRemittance Prices Worldwide - Issue 54, September 2025\nL271@P12: 13\nL272@P12: Official Use Only\nL273@P12: South Africa remains the costliest G20 country to send remittances from (see Figure 7). This is despite\nL274@P12: an overall decrease from its peak in Q1 2013, when the cost of sending from South Africa was more than\nL275@P12: 20 percent. In Q3 2025, remitting from South Africa incurred an average cost of 15.65 percent, an increase\nL276@P12: from its recorded value of 15.23 percent in Q1 2025. The cost of sending from the second most expensive\nL277@P12: G20 sending country – Brazil – was recorded at 11.91 percent in Q3 2025. Germany is the least expensive\nL278@P12: G20 sending country, recorded at 4.49 percent, followed by United Kingdom 4.61 percent, the United\nL279@P12: States (5.04 percent), Australia (5.23 percent), France (5.23 percent), Canada (5.27 percent), the Korea\nL280@P12: Rep. (5.47 percent), and Saudi Arabia (5.52 percent).\nL281@P12: Figure 7 Average cost of remitting from G20 countries\nL282@P12-13: Figure 8 Average cost of sending $200 to G20 countriesRemittance Prices Worldwide - Issue 54, September 2025\nL283@P13: 14\nL284@P13: Official Use Only\nL285@P13: Figures 8 and 9 display the total average cost of sending $200 to G20 countries over time and in Q3 2025,\nL286@P13: respectively (see also Table 4 in the Annex). The average cost of sending money to the G20 countries\nL287@P13: that are included in RPW as receiving markets was recorded at 5.68 percent in Q3 2025.\nL288@P13: Apart from a few quarters, the average cost of sending money to the G20 countries has followed the\nL289@P13: pattern of the Global Average. Since Q2 2017, the cost of remitting to G20 countries is recorded below\nL290@P13: the Global Average. In Q3 2025, China (7.31 percent) overtook South Africa as the most expensive\nL291@P13: country in this group to remit to, followed by South Africa (5.96 percent). Costs for sending remittances to\nL292@P13: India Indonesia, Türkiye, and Mexico were recorded below 6 percent. Mexico was the cheapest receiving\nL293@P13: market in the G20 group, recorded at 4.53 percent.\nL294@P13: Figure 9 Average cost of remitting to G20 countries, by Country\nL295@P13: Regional trends\nL296@P13: Cost of sending remittances by region of the world\nL297@P13: The cost for remittance services varies significantly depending on the region where the money is being\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn2414view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2413view2\",\"pattern\":\"Compromiso\"}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.ifc.org/content/dam/ifc/doc/mgrt/family-business-governance-handbook-spanish.pdf: (400) Timeout fetching--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn2414view4 [wordlim: 200] Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2413view2\",\"pattern\":\"Constitución familiar\"}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.ifc.org/content/dam/ifc/doc/mgrt/family-business-governance-handbook-spanish.pdf: (400) Timeout fetching"
